WEBVTT

00:00:00.341 --> 00:00:02.651
Last time, we balanced two M g plus

00:00:02.792 --> 00:00:04.500
O two makes two M g O.

00:00:05.122 --> 00:00:07.920
So two magnesium atoms react with one oxygen

00:00:07.982 --> 00:00:08.383
molecule.

00:00:09.066 --> 00:00:10.828
But this zero point two four of a

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:13.894
gram of ribbon holds thousands of billions of

00:00:14.056 --> 00:00:15.357
billions of magnesium atoms.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.546
Nobody can count that one at a time.

00:00:18.311 --> 00:00:19.994
And one carbon atom has a mass of

00:00:20.055 --> 00:00:22.218
about two times ten to the minus twenty-three

00:00:22.259 --> 00:00:24.523
grams, far too small for any balance.

00:00:25.427 --> 00:00:27.652
So how do chemists count particles they can't

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:28.274
even see?

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.058
They count the way we count anything in

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:31.340
bulk,

00:00:31.884 --> 00:00:32.788
with a counting unit.

00:00:33.472 --> 00:00:34.917
A dozen always means twelve,

00:00:35.541 --> 00:00:37.608
and a ream of paper is always five

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:38.191
hundred sheets.

00:00:38.855 --> 00:00:41.430
Twelve eggs or twelve watermelons, it's still a

00:00:41.471 --> 00:00:41.752
dozen.

00:00:42.416 --> 00:00:44.181
If you buy five dozen, you know you've

00:00:44.202 --> 00:00:46.047
got sixty, without counting each one.

00:00:47.071 --> 00:00:49.457
Chemists use a counting unit too, and it's

00:00:49.498 --> 00:00:50.420
called the mole.

00:00:51.509 --> 00:00:53.697
One mole is six point zero two two

00:00:53.778 --> 00:00:55.585
times ten to the twenty-three particles.

00:00:56.228 --> 00:00:58.938
That number is called the Avogadro constant, after

00:00:58.959 --> 00:01:01.267
the Italian scientist Amedeo Avogadro.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:02.370
It's written N

00:01:03.112 --> 00:01:05.095
A, and its unit is per mole,

00:01:05.637 --> 00:01:07.379
because it tells you how many particles are

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.040
in each mole.

00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:10.988
When we count a substance in moles, that's

00:01:11.109 --> 00:01:12.131
its chemical amount.

00:01:12.774 --> 00:01:14.862
Its symbol is a small n, and its

00:01:14.964 --> 00:01:16.851
unit is the mole, written m o l.

00:01:17.931 --> 00:01:19.182
How big is that number?

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:22.815
If you counted one particle every second, without

00:01:22.856 --> 00:01:23.216
stopping,

00:01:23.798 --> 00:01:27.446
one mole would take about nineteen million billion

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:27.807
years.

00:01:28.429 --> 00:01:30.324
That's more than a million times the age

00:01:30.346 --> 00:01:31.031
of the universe.

00:01:31.835 --> 00:01:34.082
Yet a mole of water molecules is only

00:01:34.123 --> 00:01:35.045
about a tablespoon.

00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:37.085
That's how small particles are.

00:01:37.978 --> 00:01:39.028
Why such an odd number?

00:01:39.735 --> 00:01:42.064
It was chosen to link counting with weighing.

00:01:42.807 --> 00:01:46.229
Twelve grams of carbon twelve contains almost exactly

00:01:46.491 --> 00:01:47.691
one mole of atoms,

00:01:48.293 --> 00:01:50.294
so a mole of particles has a mass

00:01:50.395 --> 00:01:52.737
in grams close to the relative masses you

00:01:52.798 --> 00:01:53.277
already know.

00:01:54.083 --> 00:01:55.258
We'll use that link next time.

00:01:55.763 --> 00:01:56.165
For now, a

00:01:56.669 --> 00:01:58.038
mole is simply a count.

00:01:58.796 --> 00:02:00.543
So there are two numbers to keep apart.

00:02:01.146 --> 00:02:03.596
Capital N is the number of particles,

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:05.825
a huge count with no unit.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.699
Small n is the chemical amount,

00:02:09.203 --> 00:02:09.664
in moles.

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:12.377
They're linked by the Avogadro constant.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.213
The number of particles equals the moles times

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:15.475
N A.

00:02:16.482 --> 00:02:18.671
And going back, the moles equal the number

00:02:18.692 --> 00:02:20.580
of particles divided by N A.

00:02:21.351 --> 00:02:21.898
Quick prediction.

00:02:22.829 --> 00:02:24.673
One mole of hydrogen molecules,

00:02:25.456 --> 00:02:27.661
and one mole of oxygen molecules.

00:02:28.264 --> 00:02:29.530
Which contains more molecules?

00:02:33.966 --> 00:02:34.290
Neither.

00:02:35.156 --> 00:02:37.720
Each contains six point zero two two times

00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:40.286
ten to the twenty-three molecules, because a mole

00:02:40.387 --> 00:02:40.967
is a count.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:42.928
Remember the dozen watermelons.

00:02:43.992 --> 00:02:47.036
Oxygen molecules are heavier than hydrogen molecules, so

00:02:47.098 --> 00:02:49.341
a mole of oxygen has more mass, but

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:50.122
the same number.

00:02:50.926 --> 00:02:51.893
A mole is a count,

00:02:52.419 --> 00:02:52.882
not a mass.

00:02:53.743 --> 00:02:54.172
Let's use it.

00:02:55.039 --> 00:02:57.067
How many atoms are in two point five

00:02:57.129 --> 00:02:58.173
moles of carbon?

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.512
The particles are carbon atoms.

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:03.404
N equals n times N A,

00:03:04.186 --> 00:03:06.651
so two point five times six point zero

00:03:06.712 --> 00:03:08.635
two two times ten to the twenty-three.

00:03:09.297 --> 00:03:11.307
That's about one point five times ten to

00:03:11.328 --> 00:03:12.775
the twenty-four carbon atoms.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.309
The mole unit cancels with per mole, leaving

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:17.172
a plain count.

00:03:17.905 --> 00:03:18.696
Now the other way.

00:03:19.261 --> 00:03:21.586
A sample contains three point zero one times

00:03:21.646 --> 00:03:23.550
ten to the twenty-four water molecules.

00:03:24.011 --> 00:03:24.878
How many moles is that?

00:03:25.763 --> 00:03:27.591
n equals N divided by N A.

00:03:28.313 --> 00:03:30.357
That's three point zero one times ten to

00:03:30.377 --> 00:03:33.042
the twenty-four, divided by six point zero two

00:03:33.123 --> 00:03:35.267
two times ten to the twenty-three, which is

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:37.852
five point zero zero moles of water molecules.

00:03:38.637 --> 00:03:39.100
Sense check.

00:03:39.706 --> 00:03:41.530
Three over six is a half, and ten

00:03:41.551 --> 00:03:43.376
to the twenty-four is ten times ten to

00:03:43.397 --> 00:03:46.124
the twenty-three, so about five makes sense.

00:03:46.988 --> 00:03:49.774
Always say what you're counting, because one particle

00:03:49.815 --> 00:03:50.717
can contain others.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.584
Half a mole of carbon dioxide is three

00:03:53.645 --> 00:03:55.708
point zero one times ten to the twenty-three

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:56.250
molecules.

00:03:56.792 --> 00:03:59.645
But each molecule has two oxygen atoms, so

00:03:59.666 --> 00:04:01.313
there are twice as many oxygen atoms.

00:04:01.816 --> 00:04:03.984
That's six point zero two two times ten

00:04:04.005 --> 00:04:06.333
to the twenty-three, a whole mole of oxygen

00:04:06.394 --> 00:04:06.634
atoms.

00:04:07.619 --> 00:04:10.343
Ionic compounds don't form molecules, so we count

00:04:10.484 --> 00:04:13.309
formula units, the simplest ratio of ions in

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:13.850
the formula.

00:04:14.793 --> 00:04:16.775
One mole of sodium chloride holds one mole

00:04:16.796 --> 00:04:19.180
of sodium ions and one mole of chloride

00:04:19.281 --> 00:04:21.324
ions, two moles of ions in total.

00:04:22.208 --> 00:04:24.073
This also tells us what a balanced equation

00:04:24.115 --> 00:04:24.936
means in the lab.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.549
Two magnesium atoms react with one oxygen molecule.

00:04:29.313 --> 00:04:31.559
Multiply both counts by N A, and the

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:32.543
ratio doesn't change.

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:35.491
So two moles of magnesium atoms react with

00:04:35.693 --> 00:04:37.116
one mole of oxygen molecules.

00:04:37.899 --> 00:04:40.347
The coefficients count particles, so they count moles

00:04:40.408 --> 00:04:40.608
too.

00:04:41.373 --> 00:04:42.880
A later video puts that to work.

00:04:43.521 --> 00:04:44.584
Here's the line to remember.

00:04:45.166 --> 00:04:47.735
A mole is a count, so always say

00:04:47.755 --> 00:04:48.939
a mole of what.

00:04:51.651 --> 00:04:52.055
Your turn.

00:04:52.699 --> 00:04:55.422
A balloon contains nine point zero three times

00:04:55.503 --> 00:04:57.526
ten to the twenty-two helium atoms.

00:04:58.208 --> 00:05:00.420
Calculate the chemical amount of helium in the

00:05:00.441 --> 00:05:00.702
balloon.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:02.342
Pause here to answer.

00:05:13.102 --> 00:05:15.311
n equals N divided by N A.

00:05:15.973 --> 00:05:17.755
Nine point zero three times ten to the

00:05:17.815 --> 00:05:20.497
twenty-two, divided by six point zero two two

00:05:20.557 --> 00:05:21.738
times ten to the twenty-three,

00:05:22.239 --> 00:05:24.701
is zero point one five zero moles of

00:05:24.781 --> 00:05:25.422
helium atoms.

00:05:26.162 --> 00:05:28.505
Multiplying by N A instead gives more than

00:05:28.605 --> 00:05:31.348
ten to the forty-six moles, an impossible answer.

00:05:31.911 --> 00:05:32.977
That's the slip to avoid.

00:05:35.022 --> 00:05:35.447
Last one.

00:05:36.076 --> 00:05:39.284
Calculate the number of chloride ions in zero

00:05:39.345 --> 00:05:42.453
point two zero zero moles of calcium chloride,

00:05:42.855 --> 00:05:43.877
C a C l two.

00:05:44.662 --> 00:05:45.790
Pause here to answer.

00:05:56.475 --> 00:05:59.819
Each formula unit of calcium chloride has two

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.844
chloride ions, so zero point two zero zero

00:06:02.925 --> 00:06:05.388
moles of it holds zero point four zero

00:06:05.409 --> 00:06:06.930
zero moles of chloride ions.

00:06:07.532 --> 00:06:10.078
Times N A, that's two point four one

00:06:10.179 --> 00:06:12.524
times ten to the twenty-three chloride ions.

00:06:13.386 --> 00:06:15.767
Forgetting the two gives one point two zero

00:06:15.828 --> 00:06:18.549
times ten to the twenty-three, which counts formula

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.051
units, not chloride ions.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:24.390
So the mole lets chemists count particles in

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:25.354
amounts they can handle.

00:06:26.096 --> 00:06:28.585
But in the lab, we don't count particles,

00:06:28.866 --> 00:06:29.488
we weigh them.

00:06:30.111 --> 00:06:32.367
This ribbon weighs zero point two four of

00:06:32.408 --> 00:06:32.730
a gram.

00:06:33.234 --> 00:06:35.185
How many moles of magnesium is that?

00:06:36.068 --> 00:06:38.476
Next time, we'll see how molar mass turns

00:06:38.496 --> 00:06:40.120
a measured mass into moles.

00:06:43.028 --> 00:06:43.850
Here are the key notes.

00:06:44.374 --> 00:06:45.921
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:45.962 --> 00:06:46.383
to copy them.
